#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# boolean_table

#% text
# `boolean_table(e, [x1, ..., xn])` gives the values of `e` for every assignment of `true` and `false` to the variables, from all true to all false with the last variable changing fastest. `e` may be a list or a rule of expressions.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
boolean_table(x and y, [x, y])
#% output
#= [true, false, false, false]

#% cell
boolean_table([x, y, xor(x, y)], [x, y])
#% output
#= [[true, true, false], [true, false, true], [false, true, true], [false, false, false]]

#% cell
boolean_table([x, y] -> implies(x, y), [x, y])
#% output
#= [[true, true] -> true, [true, false] -> false, [false, true] -> true, [false, false] -> true]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Variables that do not occur, and variables that are not all given (the others stay in the entries):

#% cell
[boolean_table(x or y, [x, y, z]), boolean_table(x and z, [x, y])]
#% output
#= [[true, true, true, true, true, true, false, false], [z, z, false, false]]

#% text
# An expression without variables is repeated:

#% cell
boolean_table(3, [x])
#% output
#= [3, 3]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The table has `2^n` rows, and a conjunction is true in one of them:

#% cell
[length(boolean_table(x and y and z, [x, y, z])), count(boolean_table(x and y and z, [x, y, z]), true)]
#% output
#= [8, 1]

#% text
# The truth table agrees with `boolean_function`:

#% cell
boolean_function(boolean_table(x or y, [x, y]), [x, y])
#% output
#= x or y

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The variables must be given as a list:

#% cell
boolean_table(x, x)
#% output
#= boolean_table(x, x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `is_tautology`, `is_satisfiable`, `tuples`
